+
+## Static LLM Sequence Length (`--llm-lang-tokens`)
+
+`--llm-lang-tokens` is supported by `export_ov_n1d7.py` and `export_ov_n1d7_single_ov.py`. It is optional and omitted by default, which preserves the original behavior of baking in the exact seq_len captured from the sample observation.
+
+When set, it reserves a fixed number of language-token slots so that the exported LLM, VL self-attention, and DiT (or fused DiT) all compile with one static total seq_len:
+
+```
+static_seq_len = --llm-lang-tokens + (number of visual tokens in the captured observation)
+```
+
+This lets a single exported OV model serve prompts of varying length without recompiling: at inference time, `--llm-lang-tokens` must be large enough to cover the longest language prompt you plan to run, and shorter prompts are zero-padded up to the fixed length automatically (see `ov_inference_readme.md`).
+
+Notes:
+
+- `--llm-lang-tokens` must be `>=` the number of language tokens in the captured observation used for export; otherwise export raises a `ValueError`.
+- The visual token count is fixed by the captured observation (e.g. image grid) and is added on top of `--llm-lang-tokens` automatically — you only specify the language-token reserve.
+- Padding tokens are masked out via `attention_mask` (LLM, VL self-attention) and `False` mask entries (DiT image/backbone masks), so padding does not change the output for the valid tokens.
+- The resolved static seq_len is written to `export_metadata.json` (or `*_single_ov_metadata.json`) as `llm_static_seq_len`, which the inference scripts read automatically — no separate flag is needed at inference time.
+
+Example: reserve 64 language-token slots when exporting the direct-OV full pipeline for LIBERO_PANDA:
+
+```bash
+python scripts/deployment/export_ov_n1d7.py \
+  --model-path checkpoints/GR00T-N1.7-3B \
+  --dataset-path demo_data/libero_demo \
+  --embodiment-tag LIBERO_PANDA \
+  --output-dir ~/openvino_models/libero_full_direct_static \
+  --export-mode full_pipeline \
+  --precision fp16 \
+  --device cpu \
+  --llm-lang-tokens 64
+```
+
+Same idea for the single-IR direct-OV export:
+
+```bash
+python scripts/deployment/export_ov_n1d7_single_ov.py \
+  --model-path checkpoints/GR00T-N1.7-3B \
+  --dataset-path demo_data/libero_demo \
+  --embodiment-tag LIBERO_PANDA \
+  --output-dir ~/openvino_models/libero_single_direct_ov_static \
+  --precision fp16 \
+  --device cpu \
+  --llm-lang-tokens 64
+```
 
 The CLI shape is intentionally the same between `export_ov_n1d7_single_onnx_ov.py` and `export_ov_n1d7_single_ov.py`, so commands can be switched with minimal changes. The main difference is only the conversion path:
 
